  "notes": [
    "The source's own cycle runs November 30, 2025 through November 29, 2026. Only the portion landing in calendar year 2026 (January 4 - November 29, 2026) is included here; the five 2025 Sundays (Nov 30, Dec 7, 14, 21, 28) are out of this file's calendar-year scope.",
    "The source's own header explains a standing addition for this liturgical year: per the 206th Diocesan Convention's Resolution 1 (Foster Care Advocacy in Maine), a prayer for children in foster care is to be offered every Sunday from Advent 2025 through Christ the King 2026, in addition to that week's own specific intention below. The suggested text: 'We pray for all those impacted by the imperfect systems meant to care for children who cannot remain with their families of origin. We pray especially for these children, and for judges and social workers, foster and biological families, school and community members, that healing and peace may be found.' This standing weekly addition is not repeated into every entry's `subjects` and is disclosed here instead.",
    "November 21, 2026 is printed in the source as a Saturday (not the usual Sunday) -- preserved as printed rather than corrected, since (unlike a wrong date) there is no independent way to verify whether this is deliberate (e.g. tied to a specific parish or diocesan event that day) or a typo.",
    "April 5 (Easter Day) has no parish named in the source, only the acclamation 'Christ is Risen! He is Risen Indeed!' -- recorded as a category subject rather than fabricating a parish.",
    "Most weeks combine one or more named parishes with one or more separate prayer intentions (e.g. for a ministry, a diocesan body, or a cause); both are recorded as subjects in the order printed, with prayer intentions as `category` subjects using the intention's own text (minus the source's leading 'For') as the name."
  ],
